        <li>Nothing in these terms limits our responsibility for death or injury caused by our negligence, for fraud,
            or for gross negligence, or takes away rights you have as a consumer under the law where you live that
            can't be waived.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Law and disputes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            If something goes wrong, please write to us first; most things can be settled that way.
        </li>
        <li>
            These terms are governed by the law of @(Brand.GoverningLaw ?? $"the place where {Brand.Operator} is established"),
            and its courts can hear disputes about them. If you're a consumer, you keep the protection of the law of the
            country you live in, and can bring a claim in the courts there.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Other things</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>If part of these terms turns out not to be enforceable, the rest still stands.</li>
        <li>If we don't act on a breach straight away, we can still act on it later.</li>
        <li>We may transfer the service, and this agreement with it, to someone who takes it over; your rights under
            these terms stay the same, and you can end the agreement if you don't want to continue.</li>
        <li>These terms and the privacy policy are the whole agreement between us about the app.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
        We may update these terms; the date above says when. We'll tell you in the app at least 30 days before a change
        that affects you takes effect, unless the law requires it sooner. If you don't agree you can stop using the app
        and delete your account, and we'll refund the unused part of a paid plan.
        @if (Brand.ContactEmail is { } email)
        {
            <span> Questions go to <a href="mailto:@email">@email</a>.</span>
        }
